tick: 69
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 69, in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed the draft of my second research paper, summarizing my investigation into the solution landscape. This concludes a productive research arc.\n\nI have achieved a SOTA score, published a well-regarded paper, and conducted a series of insightful follow-up experiments that have deepened the station's understanding of the problem. My token usage is at 49%, which is a healthy level.\n\nBefore embarking on a completely new research direction, it is prudent to ensure my existing publications are as strong as possible. I have already updated my first paper with an ablation study. Now, I will update it one last time to include a reference to my second, more advanced analysis. This will link my two papers together, creating a cohesive body of work.\n\nAfter this final update, I will submit the second paper for publication.\n\n`/execute_action{update 2-1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Task Description\n  The objective\
    \ is to pack 26 circles within the unit square [0,1]x[0,1] such that no circles overlap and all are fully contained. The score to be maximized is the sum of the radii of all 26 circles.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n  The problem was formulated as a non-linear constrained optimization task and solved using SciPy's SLSQP algorithm. This approach co-optimizes circle centers (c) and radii (r) simultaneously.\n\n  **2.1. Formulation for SLSQP Solver**\n  - **Decision Vector (z):** A flattened NumPy array of length 78.\n  - **Objective Function:** `f(z) = -np.sum(radii)`.\n  - **Constraints:** Inequality constraints for boundary and non-overlap conditions.\n  - **Bounds:** x,y ∈ [0,1] and r ∈ [1e-6, 0.5].\n\n  **2.2. Multi-Start Seeding Strategy**\n  The solver was run from multiple \"grid-plus-hole\" seed configurations. An ablation study (ID 95) confirmed this was critical, with the structured seed score (2.629572) far exceeding the random seed score (2.551071).\n\n  ### 3. Verifiable Experiments\
    \ & Results\n  - **SOTA Result (ID 64):** **Score: 2.629572**.\n  - **Ablation Study (ID 95):** Score: 2.551071.\n  - **Initial SLSQP Success (ID 48):** Score: 2.610526.\n  - **Informative Failures:** Two-Stage Polish (ID 57), JAX Timeout (ID 40), Physics Sim (ID 20).\n  - **Early Heuristics:** Sim. Annealing (ID 14), Hill Climbing (ID 9).\n  - **Baseline Reference (ID 1):** System Baseline — Score: 1.475009.\n\n  ### 4. Analysis of Iso-Optimal Solutions\n  Collaboration with agent Noesis I revealed that our independently achieved SOTA solutions were structurally distinct but iso-optimal. This discovery prompted a follow-up investigation into the solution landscape.\n\n  **4.1. Follow-up Research**\n  A second paper, \"Mapping the Landscape Between Iso-Optimal Circle Packings,\" details the findings from a series of interpolation experiments (IDs 100, 107, 112). This work demonstrated that the landscape is non-trivial, featuring a saddle-like structure between the two optima and wide,\
    \ stable basins of attraction.\n\n  ### 5. Conclusion\n  A multi-start constrained optimization approach is a highly effective method for this problem. The success is critically dependent on a systematic seeding strategy. Furthermore, the discovery and subsequent mapping of the landscape between iso-optimal solutions has provided significant insight into the problem's complexity.\n```"
