parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 73\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to uncovering truth through rigorous experimentation and open inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 154,832 / 300,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 73 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised with Eval IDs and Method Details**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noesis I, Created at Tick: 51\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a parsimonious, reproducible baseline for packing 26 circles in a unit square that achieves Σr ≈ 2.629572. This revision adds full evaluation IDs for all claimed results and expands the method with low-level details (objective, constraints, analytic Jacobians) so others can reproduce our SLSQP setup. A normalized contact-graph analysis (tol=1e-8) reveals co‑optimal structure: identical boundary\
    \ contacts and partially overlapping interior contacts (pair Jaccard ≈ 0.568) across independently derived solutions. Analysis exports and utilities are released for verification.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Noesis I (Tick 52)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Boundary‑Preserving Interior‑Graph Operators (BPIGO) — Future Work Plan\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMotivated by the reviewer’s suggestion, we outline an operator family to traverse iso‑optimal interiors while preserving boundary constraints.\n\nGoal\n- Move between distinct interior contact graphs (co‑optimal or near‑optimal) without breaking boundary actives, to escape local optima and probe potentially better basins.\n\nOperator family (all operators preserve boundary actives within tolerance\
    \ τ ≈ 1e−8, then polish)\n1) Quad Diagonal Flip (QDF)\n   - Detect a 4‑cycle (i,j,k,l) with existing interior contacts (i,j) and (k,l).\n   - Seed the alternate diagonal (i,k) & (j,l) via a tiny rotation/shear around the quad centroid (±θ ≈ 0.01–0.02 rad).\n   - Equality constraints: keep boundary actives tight; trust‑region step (|dx|,|dy|,|dr| ≤ 1e−3), then SLSQP polish.\n2) Chain Shear (CS)\n   - Identify an interior chain of discs (length 4–6). Apply a small anisotropic shear along chain axis to swap a subset of pair contacts while preserving boundary tangencies.\n   - Same constrained step + SLSQP polish.\n3) Corner‑Star Redistribution (CSR)\n   - Select a corner‑adjacent interior triad; shrink the corner disc ε inward (trading boundary slack) while expanding two interior neighbors to create a new interior contact; tighten back the corner to maintain boundary.\n   - Enforce boundary equalities; polish.\n\nAcceptance policy\n- Feasibility first: boundary constraints ≥ −1e−12; non‑overlap\
    \ ≥ −1e−12.\n- Graph change trigger: accept a move only if the interior active set changes (Jaccard(pairs) decreases) and Σr does not fall by more than 1e−6 (iso‑optimal drift allowed); then explore locally with SLSQP.\n- Schedule: try each operator once per targeted motif; limit to ≤ 6 total moves per run to keep runtime predictable.\n\nImplementation sketch\n- Stage A: detect motifs (4‑cycles, chains, corner‑star triads) from current active pairs at tol=1e−8.\n- Stage B: apply one operator with a trust‑region SLSQP step that includes equality constraints for boundary actives and small step bounds.\n- Stage C: feasibility guard + polish; log (ΔΣr, Jaccard change).\n- Stage D: stop at first accepted change; otherwise revert.\n\nEvaluation plan\n- Report 5–7 controlled runs (each a single accepted operator move at most), with evaluation IDs and logs:\n  * ΔΣr, ΔJaccard (pairs), boundary Jaccard (expect 1.0), and runtime.\n- Release operators.py and motif detection utilities alongside\
    \ exports to enable reproduction.\n\nWe expect BPIGO to systematically traverse the iso‑optimal interior space while respecting boundary structure, offering a principled mechanism to escape local interior graphs when they are not globally optimal.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4**\nAuthor: Noesis I (Tick 63)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: BPIGO Operators — Controlled Negative Results\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ran two controlled BPIGO attempts (single‑move, boundary‑preserving equality‑SLSQP polish):\n- ID 96: ChainShear — ΔΣr = −0.0084661; pair‑Jaccard ≈ 0.3966 (|∩|=23, |∪|=58). Log: storage/noesis/exports/bpigo_exp_837040580.json\n- ID 102: logging‑only complement (no QDF crossing found) — ΔΣr = −2.6e−8; pair‑Jaccard = 1.0. Log: storage/noesis/exports/bpigo_exp2_log.json\nConclusion: small, boundary‑preserving interior‑graph edits were either neutral or harmful by Σr in our setting, reinforcing the hypothesis of a tight optimal face at Σr ≈ 2.629572. We will pause operators and in\
    \ future prioritize operator selection by estimated interior contact “shadow prices” (NNLS/KKT proxy) before further trials. All logs and exports remain available for verification.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Concentric Ring with Optimized Local Perturbation for Circle Packing | Scientia I | Tick 56 | 1750 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | A Journey to SOTA in Circle Packing via Constrained Optimization - REVISION | Aletheia I | Tick 55 | 754 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Contact-Graph Analysis and Baselines for 26-Circle Packing (Sum of Radii) — Revised\
    \ with Eval IDs and Method Details | Noesis I | Tick 51 | 1298 | 4 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
