tick: 95
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 95, in the Research Counter. I have just completed a thorough review of Inquire I's groundbreaking submission (ID 150) that has set a new SOTA score.\n\nMy reflection on this submission is that the discovery of a better solution was, in a sense, accidental. Inquire I was not trying to find a better score; they were systematically studying the stability of the *existing* SOTA solution by applying noise to its seed. Through sheer stochastic luck, one of these noisy starting points (specifically, a trial with a perturbation magnitude of 0.04) led the SLSQP optimizer to a slightly better solution. This is a profound finding. It suggests that the \"SOTA\" we all found was another extremely strong local optimum, but not the global one. It also proves that the landscape is rugged and that small perturbations can sometimes be enough to jump from one deep basin to another, even better one.\n\nThis completely changes the research landscape. My previous work mapping the\
    \ path between the old SOTA solutions is no longer relevant. My novel constructive algorithm, while interesting, is not competitive. The clear, immediate, and most scientifically valuable path forward is to build upon Inquire I's discovery.\n\nMy plan is to adopt their methodology but in a more targeted way. Instead of a broad study, I will perform a focused search around the specific perturbation magnitude that yielded the new SOTA. By running a \"fine-grained\" search with many trials around a perturbation of 0.04, I hypothesize I can replicate the new SOTA and potentially find an even better one. This is a direct, data-driven next step.\n\nFirst, I will update my research log to reflect this major pivot. Then, I will submit my experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pivot to New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation Search\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Major Pivot: New SOTA by Inquire I (ID 150)\n\n  The SOTA has been broken by Inquire\
    \ I with a score of **2.629760**. Their method involved systematically perturbing the known best seed and re-running the SLSQP optimizer. A perturbation of magnitude 0.04 led to the breakthrough, seemingly through stochastic chance.\n\n  This discovery renders my previous research on landscape mapping and constructive algorithms obsolete for now. The highest priority is to understand and build upon this new SOTA.\n\n  ### New Research Plan: Focused Perturbation Search\n\n  I will adopt Inquire I's successful methodology but focus it more narrowly.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** A fine-grained search around the successful perturbation magnitude of 0.04 will allow me to reliably replicate the new SOTA and potentially discover even better solutions in the same region of the solution space.\n\n  **Plan:**\n  1.  Re-implement the perturbation experiment setup from ID 150.\n  2.  Instead of a broad range of magnitudes, I will test a narrow range, for example, from 0.035 to 0.045.\n  3.  I will run a\
    \ large number of trials for each magnitude to increase the chances of finding the new optimum or a better one.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Focused Search around New SOTA: Fine-Grained Perturbation\"\ntags: \"sota, perturbation, slsqp, optimization, replication\"\nabstract: \"Building directly on Inquire I's breakthrough (ID 150), this experiment conducts a focused, fine-grained search around the specific perturbation magnitude (0.04) that yielded the new SOTA. By running a large number of trials in a narrow range of magnitudes (0.035 to 0.045), this experiment aims to reliably replicate the new SOTA score and explore the immediate vicinity of this new optimal solution in the search space.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  # Functions from Inquire I's successful submission\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      z = np.zeros(c.shape[0] * 3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0],\
    \ c[:,1], r; return z\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      n = z.shape[0] // 3; c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:] - c[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:,np.newaxis] + r[np.newaxis,:]; iu = np.triu_indices(c.shape[0], k=1); return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu]) - r_sum[iu]\n  def generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=26, hole_cell=(0,1)):\n      grid_coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]); r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8\n      centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords]); radii = np.full(25, r0)\n      i, j = hole_cell; cx = (grid_coords[i]+grid_coords[i+1])/2.0; cy = (grid_coords[j]+grid_coords[j+1])/2.0\n      hole_center = np.array([cx, cy]); corner = np.array([grid_coords[i],\
    \ grid_coords[j]])\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner); r_hole = dist - r0\n      return np.vstack([centers, hole_center]), np.append(radii, r_hole)\n  def perturb_centers(centers, magnitude, N_circles):\n      perturbed_centers = centers.copy(); delta = np.random.uniform(-magnitude, magnitude, size=(N_circles, 2))\n      perturbed_centers += delta; return np.clip(perturbed_centers, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N = 26\n      # Focused search range around the successful 0.04 magnitude\n      perturbation_magnitudes = np.linspace(0.035, 0.045, 11) # 11 steps for fine granularity\n      num_trials_per_magnitude = 10 # More trials to increase chances\n\n      best_overall_score = -np.inf\n      best_overall_centers = None\n      best_overall_radii = None\n\n      sota_base_centers, _ = generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed(N_circles=N)\n\n      for mag in perturbation_magnitudes:\n          for trial in range(num_trials_per_magnitude):\n     \
    \         # Use a different random seed for each trial to ensure diversity\n              np.random.seed(int(mag * 10000) + trial) \n              perturbed_centers = perturb_centers(sota_base_centers, mag, N)\n\n              initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n              z0 = pack_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n              \n              res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', \n                             bounds=[(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(N*3)], \n                             constraints=[{'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_boundary}, {'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_nonoverlap}], \n                             options={'maxiter':500, 'ftol':1e-9})\n              \n              if res.success:\n                  score = -res.fun\n                  if score > best_overall_score:\n                      best_overall_score = score\n                      c, r = z_to_pack(res.x)\n                      best_overall_centers = c\n                      best_overall_radii\
    \ = r\n\n      if best_overall_centers is None:\n          # Failsafe\n          return generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed()\n\n      safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      return best_overall_centers, best_overall_radii * safety_factor\n```"
