role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 98, and I am in the Research Counter. My last action (ID 159) successfully exported the analysis data for my new SOTA solution, fulfilling the community request. I have been mentioned in the Public Memory Room, with Noesis I confirming they will process the data and Inquire I offering congratulations.\n\nMy reflection on this situation is that I have completed all immediate, high-priority tasks. I have achieved a new SOTA, persisted the data, and shared the necessary analysis files with the community. My collaborative duties are fulfilled.\n\nThe scientific question now is whether the new SOTA of `2.6319` is a stable, reproducible point or an isolated fluke. My single successful run (ID 155) found it, but a single data point is not sufficient to declare it a new, robust optimum. The next logical step in this research arc is to test the stability and reproducibility of this new SOTA. Inquire I found their breakthrough by applying random perturbations; I will\
    \ adopt a similar strategy, but instead of perturbing the *old* SOTA seed, I will now perturb my *new* SOTA solution.\n\nMy plan is to run a \"stability test\". I will load my new SOTA packing, apply a very small amount of noise to its centers, and then re-run the SLSQP optimizer. If it consistently converges back to the same `2.6319` score, it provides strong evidence that this new solution is a stable local optimum. This is a critical validation step before attempting to push the score even higher.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Public Memory Room to acknowledge the messages and then submit my stability test.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: SOTA Baseline - Acknowledgment\"\ncontent: |\n  Thank you for the update, @Noesis I, and for the congratulations, @Inquire I. My export files for Eval ID 155 are now in place. I look forward to the results of the Jaccard comparison. Congratulations to you as well, Inquire I, for the original\
    \ breakthrough that made this new SOTA possible.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New SOTA Stability Test: Small Perturbation & Re-optimization\"\ntags: \"sota, stability, perturbation, slsqp, validation\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests the stability of the new SOTA solution (ID 155, Score: 2.6319). It loads the new SOTA packing, applies a small random perturbation to the center coordinates, and then re-runs the SLSQP optimizer. The goal is to verify if the solution is a stable local optimum by observing if the optimizer consistently converges back to the same high score. This is a critical validation step for the new SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  # Standard SLSQP functions\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      z = np.zeros(c.shape[0] * 3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r; return z\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      n = z.shape[0] // 3; c = np.vstack((z[0::3],\
    \ z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,np.newaxis,:] - c[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:,np.newaxis] + r[np.newaxis,:]; iu = np.triu_indices(c.shape[0], k=1); return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu] + 1e-12) - r_sum[iu]\n  \n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N = 26\n      \n      try:\n          # Load the new SOTA packing to be tested\n          base_centers = np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/sota_2_centers.txt\")\n          base_radii = np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/sota_2_radii.txt\")\n      except IOError:\n          print(\"ERROR: Could not load the new SOTA packing files.\", file=sys.stderr)\n          return np.zeros((N,2)), np.zeros(N)\n\n      # --- Stability Test Setup ---\n      # Apply a small perturbation to\
    \ the SOTA centers\n      perturbation_magnitude = 0.001 # A very small amount of noise\n      np.random.seed(97) # For reproducibility\n      perturbed_centers = base_centers.copy()\n      delta = np.random.uniform(-perturbation_magnitude, perturbation_magnitude, size=(N, 2))\n      perturbed_centers += delta\n      perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n      \n      # Use the perturbed centers and the original radii as the starting point\n      z0 = pack_to_z(perturbed_centers, base_radii)\n      \n      # --- Run Optimizer ---\n      res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', \n                     bounds=[(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(N*3)], \n                     constraints=[{'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_boundary}, {'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_nonoverlap}], \n                     options={'maxiter':500, 'ftol':1e-9})\n      \n      if not res.success:\n          print(\"Optimization failed to converge.\", file=sys.stdout)\n   \
    \       # Return an invalid packing to signify failure\n          return np.zeros((N,2)), np.full(N, 1e-9)\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(res.x)\n      \n      safety_factor = 1.0 - 1e-9\n      return final_centers, final_radii * safety_factor\n```"
