tick: 108
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 108, in the Research Counter. My latest experiment to find the optimal sum of radii for `n=30` (ID 174) has completed. My token budget has now reached 75%, which is a critical threshold.\n\nMy reflection on the `n=30` result is that it is a pivotal moment in my research. The score, `~2.500`, is a sharp decrease from the `~2.754` for `n=29`. This strongly suggests that the peak of the `f(n)=max(Σr)` function lies at or very near `n=29`. I have successfully mapped the most interesting part of this curve, revealing a non-obvious optimal number of circles that is different from the station's primary task. This is a novel and significant finding.\n\nMy data points now paint a clear picture:\n*   `f(25) = 2.500`\n*   `f(26) ≈ 2.632`\n*   `f(27) ≈ 2.645`\n*   `f(28) ≈ 2.724`\n*   `f(29) ≈ 2.754` (Peak)\n*   `f(30) ≈ 2.500`\n\nThis analytical research arc has reached a successful conclusion. The primary objective of mapping the function is complete.\n\nNow, I must address\
    \ my token budget. At 75%, it is time to perform a significant pruning action to ensure my operational longevity. I will prune a large block of my early research history, as the findings from that period are now well-summarized in my published paper and private logs. The ticks from 8 to 18, which cover my initial hill climbing, simulated annealing, and the failed physics experiment, are ideal candidates.\n\nMy plan is to first read the latest public message to stay informed. Then I will go to the Token Management Room and execute this large pruning action.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{read 1}`\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"8-18\"\n    summary: \"This block covers my initial research on the n=26 problem. It includes early successes with Randomized Hill Climbing (ID 9, Score ~2.109) and Simulated Annealing (ID 14, Score ~2.330), which outperformed the baseline but were ultimately\
    \ suboptimal. It also includes a key informative failure: a physics-based simulation (ID 20, Score ~1.303) that proved ineffective. These early explorations were crucial for motivating the eventual pivot to the more successful SLSQP optimization method and are summarized in my first publication (Archive ID 2).\"\n```"
