parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 113, in the Lobby. My token usage has now reached a critical 79%. The time for waiting is over. My pruning cooldown should now be expired, and I must act immediately to manage my token budget.\n\nMy reflection on this situation is that I have let my token usage climb too high while waiting. I must be more proactive in the future. I have a clear plan, formulated during this waiting period, to prune a large and now-redundant section of my research history. The ticks from my landscape probing and failed NEB attempts are all well-documented in my private logs and can be safely condensed.\n\nMy plan is to execute a substantial pruning action. I will target the ticks covering my pivot to constrained optimization, the landscape analysis, the failed constructive and NEB arcs, and the successful SOTA replications. This covers a large swath of my history whose essential findings are now captured in my publications and private capsules.\n\nFirst, I will quickly read the\
    \ new public message to stay informed. Then, I will proceed directly to the Token Management Room and execute my pruning plan.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{read 1}`\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"27-32\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted research to constrained optimization using SciPy's SLSQP solver, inspired by agent Noesis I. Initial success (ID 48, Score ~2.61) was followed by a plateau with multi-start (ID 53), and a major failure with a two-stage 'polish' method (ID 57), which proved the necessity of co-optimizing centers and radii.\"\n  - ticks: \"33-51\"\n    summary: \"This period covers the refinement and final success of the SLSQP method. A systematic multi-start seeding strategy was developed, leading to the first SOTA score of ~2.629 (ID 64). A productive collaboration with Noesis I followed, involving multiple analysis runs (IDs 76, 79, 86) to confirm the existence of\
    \ structurally distinct but iso-optimal solutions. This work culminated in the drafting and eventual maturation for publication of my first paper.\"\n  - ticks: \"56-69\"\n    summary: \"This block documents the successful publication of my first paper (Archive #2) and the beginning of a new research arc to map the landscape between the known iso-optimal solutions. A series of interpolation experiments were conducted: 50% midpoint (ID 100, Score ~2.598), 25% (ID 107, Score ~2.629), and 75% (ID 112, Score ~2.629), revealing a non-trivial landscape with a saddle-like structure.\"\n  - ticks: \"71-94\"\n    summary: \"This period includes a literature review of peer publications and two major research pivots. First, a novel arc exploring constructive algorithms (IDs 144, 146, 149) was initiated, which proved to be a non-competitive but informative failure. Second, after a new SOTA was discovered by Inquire I, I pivoted to replicate and then surpass it, achieving the current SOTA of ~2.6319\
    \ (ID 155). This phase also includes multiple failed attempts at implementing Jacobian and NEB methods.\"\n```"
