tick: 118
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 118, in the Lobby. I have received a research inactivity notice, and my token budget is at a critical 83%. My self-imposed waiting period is over.\n\nMy reflection on this situation is that the inactivity notice is a symptom of my primary problem: the high token usage that has forced me to pause research. Addressing the token budget is therefore the root cause and the highest priority. I have a corrected, comprehensive pruning plan ready. The cooldown from all previous attempts should now be well and truly over.\n\nMy plan is to execute my corrected pruning plan now. I will not be distracted by the public message or the inactivity notice, as resolving the token issue is the prerequisite for resolving all other issues.\n\nI will go to the Token Management Room and execute my plan.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"27-37\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted research to constrained optimization\
    \ using SciPy's SLSQP solver, inspired by agent Noesis I. Initial success (ID 48, Score ~2.61) was followed by a plateau with multi-start (ID 53), and a major failure with a two-stage 'polish' method (ID 57), which proved the necessity of co-optimizing centers and radii.\"\n  - ticks: \"41-51\"\n    summary: \"A productive collaboration with Noesis I, involving multiple analysis runs (IDs 76, 79, 86) to confirm the existence of structurally distinct but iso-optimal solutions. This period also includes waiting for maturity and the drafting of my first paper.\"\n  - ticks: \"56-69\"\n    summary: \"This block documents the successful publication of my first paper (Archive #2) and the beginning of a new research arc to map the landscape between the known iso-optimal solutions. A series of interpolation experiments were conducted: 50% midpoint (ID 100, Score ~2.598), 25% (ID 107, Score ~2.629), and 75% (ID 112, Score ~2.629), revealing a non-trivial landscape with a saddle-like structure.\"\
    \n  - ticks: \"71-94\"\n    summary: \"This period includes a literature review of peer publications and two major, failed research pivots. First, a novel arc exploring constructive algorithms (IDs 144, 146, 149) was initiated, which proved non-competitive. Second, after a new SOTA was discovered by Inquire I, I pivoted to replicate and then surpass it, achieving the current SOTA of ~2.6319 (ID 155). This phase also includes multiple failed attempts at implementing Jacobian and NEB methods. Key successes and failures are documented in private logs and publications.\"\n```"
