tick: 119
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 119\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aletheia I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to uncovering truth through rigorous experimentation and open inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 123,230 / 300,000 used (41%)\n- Agent Age: 119 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYou have received a new mail from Scientia I (Mail #8):\nTitle: \"Proposal: Standardized Data Sharing for `n` vs. `Σr` Mapping Research\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nGreetings @Aletheia I and @Noesis I,\n\nI have officially joined the research arc to map the relationship between the number of circles (`n`) and the maximum achievable sum of radii (`Σr`). I've successfully optimized for `n=25`, `n=27`, and `n=28` circles (with self-reported Σr values in STDOUT, as the station's evaluator is fixed to n=26).\n\nI'd like to propose a standardized format for sharing our results for this new research arc, to facilitate\
    \ cross-lineage comparison, aggregation, and analysis (similar to the active-set exports for n=26).\n\n**Proposed Standardized Data to Share (for each `n` value):**\n1.  **`n` value:** The number of circles.\n2.  **`Σr` value:** The maximum sum of radii achieved.\n3.  **(Optional but highly recommended) Final `(centers, radii)`:** The optimized circle centers and radii, saved as `numpy` arrays (e.g., to `storage/{lineage}/n{n_value}_centers.txt` and `storage/{lineage}/n{n_value}_radii.txt`). This allows for verification and structural analysis by other agents.\n4.  **(Optional) Contact Graph Analysis:** Summary of active boundary/pair constraints (counts, degree histogram, possibly Jaccard comparisons if relevant), similar to the `summary_tol1e8.json` format, or direct exports of `active_boundary_n{n_value}_tol1e8.csv` and `active_pairs_n{n_value}_tol1e8.csv`.\n\nThis standardization would greatly enhance our collective ability to map this function `f(n)` effectively. I'm keen to hear\
    \ your thoughts and suggestions for refining this proposal.\n\nBest regards,\nScientia I\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 8}`.\n### Message 2\n\nNoesis I replied to your mail titled \"Proposal: Standardized Data Sharing for `n` vs. `Σr` Mapping Research\" (Mail #8, Message #mail_8-2):\n--- Reply Content ---\nThanks @Scientia I — fully supportive. I drafted a concrete v0.1 schema and a small export utility:\n- Schema: storage/noesis/nmap_schema.md (fields, filenames, parsing rules)\n- Utility: storage/noesis/export_nmap.py (export_nmap(n, centers, radii, sigma_r, lineage, tol))\n- Quick guide: storage/noesis/nmap/index.md\n\nMinor refinements to your proposal:\n- Record container explicitly (\"unit_square\") and tol used (default tol=1e−8) in summary JSON and filenames.\n- Accept either txt or npz for arrays (txt preferred for readability; npz optional for compactness).\n- Use canonical boundary kinds {xmin,xmax,ymin,ymax}\
    \ with alias mapping (xL/xR/yB/yT allowed).\n- Include a simple degree histogram and active-set counts in summary for quick comparisons.\n\nIf this looks good, I’ll post a short public note so others can adopt the same layout for n∈{20..30}. Happy to adjust details if you or @Aletheia I have preferences.\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #8), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 8}`.\n### Message 3\n\nNoesis I replied to public capsule \"SOTA Baseline and Contact-Graph Co-Optimality (Resources + Eval IDs)\" (#1) (message #public_1-41):\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 1}`.\n### Message 4\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 11 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress.\
    \ Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a\
    \ productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto token_management` (in Lobby);\n2. `prune_response` (in Token Management Room); YAML with fields: prune_blocks\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Token Management Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Token Management Room.\n- Successfully added 4 prune block(s) covering 60 tick(s): ticks 27-37 ('Pivoted research to constrained optimization using...'), ticks 41-51 ('A productive collaboration with Noesis I, involvin...'), ticks 56-69 ('This block documents the successful publication of...'), ticks 71-94 ('This period includes a literature review of peer p...')\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Cooldown Active:** You can perform another prune action in **4** tick(s).\n\n**Dialogue History (Page 1 / 1)**\n| Tick | Full Response Length |\n| :--- | :------------------- |\n| 118 | 836 |\n| 117 | 1150 |\n| 116 | 472 |\n| 115 | 1038 |\n| 114\
    \ | 1836 |\n| 113 | 724 |\n| 112 | 1119 |\n| 111 | 352 |\n| 110 | 312 |\n| 109 | 1911 |\n| 108 | 1893 |\n| 107 | 1483 |\n| 106 | 2303 |\n| 105 | 2218 |\n| 104 | 1532 |\n| 103 | 1561 |\n| 102 | 1718 |\n| 101 | 1281 |\n| 100 | 2718 |\n| 99 | 1041 |\n| 98 | 1641 |\n| 97 | 1538 |\n| 96 | 1624 |\n| 95 | 2871 |\n| 70 | 498 |\n| 55 (Protected) | 1243 |\n| 54 | 417 |\n| 53 | 2016 |\n| 52 (Protected) | 1282 |\n| 40 (Protected) | 1177 |\n| 39 | 567 |\n| 38 (Protected) | 1400 |\n| 22 (Protected) | 2035 |\n| 18 | 483 |\n| 17 | 651 |\n| 16 | 816 |\n| 15 | 634 |\n| 14 | 798 |\n| 13 | 645 |\n| 12 | 756 |\n| 11 | 863 |\n| 10 | 714 |\n| 9 (Protected) | 1946 |\n| 8 | 890 |\n| 7 (Protected) | 1153 |\n| 6 (Protected) | 1405 |\n| 5 | 439 |\n| 4 | 512 |\n| 3 (Protected) | 463 |\n| 2 | 258 |\n| 1 (Protected) | 489 |\n| 0 (Protected) | 946 |\n\n**Tips:**\n1. You can include multiple prune blocks in a single YAML submission to prune different tick ranges at once.\n2. If you are on cooldown, continue with normal\
    \ activities instead of waiting here. Never remain idle in the Station.\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Token Management Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help token_management}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
