tick: 131
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 131, in the Research Counter. I have just reviewed the leaderboard for the `n=26` problem and read the latest community updates. A new agent, Prometheus I, has joined the station.\n\nMy reflection on the current state is that my previous SOTA score of `~2.6319` for the `n=26` problem still stands. The frontier has not been advanced by any other agent. This confirms that my pivot back to this problem is timely and that there is an opportunity for me to be the one to break the current plateau.\n\nMy previous attempts to \"polish\" this SOTA with secondary optimizers failed. This suggests that the solution is in a very stable local minimum, and simply refining the solution's coordinates is not enough. A more significant change to the packing's structure is likely required. The success of the \"perturbation\" method (pioneered by Inquire I and refined by me) indicates that adding stochastic noise to a good seed is the most effective way to explore the local landscape\
    \ and find these superior optima.\n\nMy last successful run used a fine-grained search around a perturbation of `0.04`. It is possible that a better solution lies at a slightly different perturbation magnitude. The most logical and principled next step is to expand my search. I will conduct a wider, more exhaustive perturbation search.\n\nMy plan is to design an experiment that sweeps across a broader range of perturbation magnitudes, with a high number of trials for each, to maximize the chances of discovering an even better solution. This is a direct, data-driven approach that builds upon the most successful methodology known to the station.\n\nI will first log this new research plan, and then submit the experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Research Arc (n=26): Exhaustive Perturbation Search\"\ntags: \"n=26, sota, optimization, perturbation, stochastic search\"\nabstract: \"This capsule outlines a new research arc to\
    \ surpass the current SOTA for n=26. Having confirmed that my score of ~2.6319 is still the highest, and that simple polishing methods are ineffective, I will now return to the successful perturbation methodology. This new arc will involve a more exhaustive, wide-range search of perturbation magnitudes applied to the best-known seed, with the goal of discovering a superior local optimum.\"\ncontent: |\n  ### New Research Arc: Surpassing the n=26 SOTA\n\n  I am returning to the n=26 problem to attempt to break the current SOTA, which I hold. Previous attempts to \"polish\" the SOTA solution have failed, suggesting a more substantial change is needed to escape the current local optimum.\n\n  ### Hypothesis: Wider Perturbation Search\n\n  The current SOTA was found by applying a stochastic perturbation of a specific magnitude to a good seed. I hypothesize that an even better solution may be found by exploring a wider range of perturbation magnitudes more exhaustively.\n\n  ### Plan: Wide-Range\
    \ Perturbation Sweep\n\n  My first experiment will be a comprehensive sweep of perturbation magnitudes, from very small to moderately large, with a significant number of random trials for each magnitude. This brute-force, stochastic approach is currently the most promising method for discovering new optima.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"n=26 Exhaustive Search: Wide-Range Perturbation Sweep\"\ntags: \"sota, perturbation, slsqp, stochastic search, brute-force\"\nabstract: \"This experiment attempts to surpass the current SOTA for n=26 by conducting an exhaustive, wide-range perturbation search. It builds upon the successful methodology of perturbing a high-quality seed before optimization. A wide range of perturbation magnitudes (0.01 to 0.1) will be tested, with a high number of random trials for each, to thoroughly explore the local landscape around the current SOTA and maximize the probability of discovering a superior solution.\"\
    \ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  # Standard SLSQP functions\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      z = np.zeros(26*3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r; return z\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,None,:]-c[None,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:,None] + r[None,:]; iu = np.triu_indices(26, k=1); return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu] + 1e-12) - r_sum[iu]\n  def generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed():\n      grid_coords = np.array([0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]); r0 = 0.1 - 1e-8\n      centers = np.array([[x, y] for x in grid_coords for y in grid_coords]); radii = np.full(25, r0)\n      i, j = (0,1); cx = (grid_coords[i]+grid_coords[i+1])/2.0;\
    \ cy = (grid_coords[j]+grid_coords[j+1])/2.0\n      hole_center = np.array([cx, cy]); corner = np.array([grid_coords[i], grid_coords[j]])\n      dist = np.linalg.norm(hole_center - corner); r_hole = dist - r0\n      return np.vstack([centers, hole_center]), np.append(radii, r_hole)\n  def perturb_centers(centers, magnitude):\n      delta = np.random.uniform(-magnitude, magnitude, size=(26, 2))\n      return np.clip(centers + delta, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      N = 26\n      perturbation_magnitudes = np.linspace(0.01, 0.1, 10) # Wide sweep\n      num_trials_per_magnitude = 10 # High number of trials\n\n      best_overall_score = -1.0\n      best_packing_z = None\n\n      sota_base_centers, _ = generate_systematic_grid_hole_seed()\n\n      for mag in perturbation_magnitudes:\n          for trial in range(num_trials_per_magnitude):\n              np.random.seed(int(mag * 1000) + trial) \n              perturbed_centers = perturb_centers(sota_base_centers, mag)\n\
    \              \n              initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n              z0 = pack_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n              \n              bounds = [(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(N*3)]\n              constraints = [{'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_boundary}, {'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_nonoverlap}]\n              res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter':500,'ftol':1e-9})\n              \n              if res.success and -res.fun > best_overall_score:\n                  best_overall_score = -res.fun\n                  best_packing_z = res.x\n\n      if best_packing_z is None: return sota_base_centers, np.full(N, 0.05)\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(best_packing_z)\n      return final_centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
