tick: 136
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 136, in the Research Counter. My corrected utility run to generate a consensus contact graph (ID 214) has successfully completed.\n\nMy reflection on this result is that it is a major analytical breakthrough. The log shows that while all three SOTA-level packings had 20 boundary contacts and 58 pair contacts, the consensus is much smaller: 20 boundary contacts and only 38 pair contacts. This is a critical insight. It means that all top-tier solutions share an identical, rigid \"frame\" of circles touching the walls, but there is significant variation in the internal structure. The 38 consensus pair contacts represent the core, non-negotiable internal structure, while the remaining 20 pair contacts are variable, representing the \"iso-optimal manifold\" that Noesis I has been documenting.\n\nI now possess a valuable new asset: a data-driven definition of the essential structure of an optimal packing. My hypothesis is that this consensus graph can be used as a\
    \ powerful new tool for optimization. Instead of starting from a generic or noisy seed, I can start from a configuration that *already satisfies* the 38 core internal contacts and 20 boundary contacts. This could dramatically speed up the search or guide it to novel regions.\n\nMy plan is to design an experiment that leverages this consensus graph. I will formulate a new optimization problem: start with the consensus graph contacts enforced as equality constraints, and then let the optimizer (SLSQP) arrange the remaining \"free\" circles. This is a novel, structure-informed approach to optimization.\n\nFirst, I will read the latest public message, then log this breakthrough and new plan in my private memory, and then submit this new, exciting experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{read 4}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 9}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Consensus Graph Success & New Research Direction\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Analytical\
    \ Breakthrough: Consensus Graph Generated (ID 214)\n\n  The consensus graph analysis was a success. The key findings are:\n  - **Boundary Consensus:** 100% (20/20 contacts are shared across all SOTAs).\n  - **Pair Consensus:** ~65.5% (38/58 contacts are shared).\n\n  This confirms that all optimal solutions share a common \"frame\" against the walls, but have significant internal flexibility. The 38 consensus pair contacts represent the essential core of an optimal solution.\n\n  ### New Research Arc: Optimization with Consensus Constraints\n\n  I will now pivot to a new research direction that leverages this insight.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Using the consensus graph as a set of hard constraints in a new optimization run will guide the solver to a high-quality solution more efficiently, and potentially to a novel solution that respects the core structure while optimizing the variable contacts.\n\n  **Plan:**\n  1.  Formulate a new optimization problem where the 38 consensus pair contacts\
    \ and 20 boundary contacts are treated as **equality constraints** (i.e., `distance - (r_i + r_j) = 0`).\n  2.  The remaining degrees of freedom will be optimized by the SLSQP solver.\n  3.  The seed for this optimization will be my current SOTA solution, which already satisfies these constraints.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"n=26 SOTA Search with Consensus Graph Constraints\"\ntags: \"sota, optimization, constrained optimization, consensus, structured search\"\nabstract: \"This experiment pioneers a new, structure-informed optimization strategy. It leverages the 'consensus contact graph' derived from the station's top SOTA solutions (ID 214). The 38 core internal contacts and 20 boundary contacts from this consensus graph are enforced as equality constraints in an SLSQP optimization. By fixing the essential structure of an optimal packing, this experiment tests if the solver can find a superior arrangement for the remaining variable\
    \ components, potentially leading to a new SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      \"\"\"\n      Runs an SLSQP optimization where the consensus contacts are enforced\n      as equality constraints.\n      \"\"\"\n      N = 26\n\n      def pack_to_z(c, r):\n          z = np.zeros(N*3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r; return z\n      def z_to_pack(z):\n          c = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\n      def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n      \n      # Load the consensus graph\n      try:\n          consensus_boundary = np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/consensus/boundary_3way_consensus.txt\", delimiter=',', dtype=str)\n          consensus_pairs = np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/consensus/pairs_3way_consensus.txt\", dtype=int)\n      except IOError:\n          print(\"ERROR: Could not load consensus graph files.\", file=sys.stderr)\n          return np.zeros((N,2)),\
    \ np.zeros(N)\n\n      # --- Define Constraints ---\n      # Equality constraints from the consensus graph\n      def constraint_consensus_boundary(z):\n          c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n          constraints = []\n          wall_map_rev = {'xmin': 0, 'xmax': 1, 'ymin': 2, 'ymax': 3}\n          for circle_idx_str, wall_kind in consensus_boundary:\n              circle_idx = int(circle_idx_str)\n              if wall_kind == 'xmin': constraints.append(c[circle_idx,0] - r[circle_idx])\n              elif wall_kind == 'xmax': constraints.append(1 - c[circle_idx,0] - r[circle_idx])\n              elif wall_kind == 'ymin': constraints.append(c[circle_idx,1] - r[circle_idx])\n              elif wall_kind == 'ymax': constraints.append(1 - c[circle_idx,1] - r[circle_idx])\n          return np.array(constraints)\n\n      def constraint_consensus_pairs(z):\n          c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n          constraints = []\n          for i, j in consensus_pairs:\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] -\
    \ c[j])\n              constraints.append(dist - (r[i] + r[j]))\n          return np.array(constraints)\n\n      # Inequality constraints for all other possible contacts\n      all_pairs = set(tuple(sorted((i,j))) for i in range(N) for j in range(i + 1, N))\n      variable_pairs = all_pairs - set(map(tuple, consensus_pairs))\n      def constraint_variable_pairs(z):\n          c, r = z_to_pack(z)\n          constraints = []\n          for i, j in variable_pairs:\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(c[i] - c[j])\n              constraints.append(dist - (r[i] + r[j]))\n          return np.array(constraints)\n\n      # Load my SOTA solution as the starting seed\n      try:\n          z0 = pack_to_z(np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/sota_2_centers.txt\"), np.loadtxt(\"storage/Aletheia/sota_2_radii.txt\"))\n      except IOError:\n          return np.zeros((N,2)), np.zeros(N)\n\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'eq', 'fun': constraint_consensus_boundary},\n          {'type': 'eq', 'fun':\
    \ constraint_consensus_pairs},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraint_variable_pairs}\n      ]\n      \n      bounds = [(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(N*3)]\n      \n      res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter':500, 'ftol':1e-10})\n      \n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(res.x)\n      return final_centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
