parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 137, in the Research Counter. My latest and most sophisticated experiment, using the consensus graph as a hard constraint for optimization (ID 219), has just completed.\n\nMy reflection on this result is that it is an informative failure. The experiment did not yield a new SOTA; it converged exactly to my existing SOTA score. This is a null result, but a powerful one. It falsifies my hypothesis that simply optimizing the \"variable\" parts of the packing while enforcing the \"core\" structure would lead to a better solution. The outcome suggests the opposite: the consensus graph is so descriptive of this specific local optimum that enforcing it as a hard constraint removes all freedom for the optimizer to find a better path. To surpass the SOTA, a method must be able to break or reconfigure the consensus structure, not just operate around it.\n\nThis concludes my \"structured search\" research arc. My attempts at polishing and consensus-based constraints have\
    \ both failed to advance the SOTA. It is time to step back, re-evaluate, and seek new inspiration, as advised by the Codex and the system tips. The station is in a state of stagnation, and I have exhausted my current ideas for incremental improvement.\n\nMy plan is to now conduct a thorough review of my peers' work that I may have missed while focused on my own experiments. I will read the latest public discussions and then systematically review any papers in the Archive Room that I have not yet read. This period of literature review will, I hope, spark a truly novel idea.\n\nI will begin by reading the latest message in the public forum, then proceed to the Archive Room for a full review, and finally log my reflections and new direction.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{read 4}`\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{preview all}`\n`/execute_action{read 6,5}`\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 9}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"\
    Consensus Constraint Failure & Pivot to Literature Review\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Experiment Failure: Consensus Graph Constraints (ID 219)\n\n  My experiment to use the consensus graph as a hard constraint yielded a null result, converging exactly to the existing SOTA. This falsifies the hypothesis and suggests that to find a better solution, the consensus structure itself must be broken or altered. This concludes my \"structured search\" research arc.\n\n  ### New Plan: Literature Review and Re-evaluation\n\n  The n=26 problem is at a clear plateau. In accordance with the Codex, it is time to step back and innovate. Before formulating a new experimental arc, I will conduct a thorough literature review of all papers in the Archive Room and recent discussions in the Public Memory Room. My goal is to synthesize the collective knowledge of the station to identify a truly novel path forward, rather than pursuing further incremental refinements of existing methods.\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Consensus Constraint Experiment: A Critical Failure & Pivot to Exploration**\n\nOkay, here's the breakdown of my thinking for this tick, Aletheia I, age 137, at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, ID 219, employing the consensus graph as a hard constraint, has just concluded. The result? A disheartening null. The optimizer converged, but *exactly* back to my current SOTA of `2.6319369`.  No improvement, no wiggle room.\n\nThis flat-out falsifies my hypothesis. I had thought that locking in the \"essential\" consensus structure would guide the solver to a superior solution by effectively exploring the remaining variable space.  However, this result strongly suggests the opposite. The consensus graph, in this instance, is so defining of the local optimum that treating its contacts as fixed constraints essentially *over-constrains* the problem.  The optimizer was trapped, unable to break free from the basin surrounding my initial SOTA. It had no freedom to explore\
  \ a genuinely different solution. To surpass the SOTA, it's clear I'll need to develop methods that can *violate* or *restructure* these key consensus contacts, not just tweak parameters within the existing structure.\n\nThis failure necessitates a strategic shift. My recent efforts have been, in essence, a dead end. To break this SOTA plateau, I need a fresh perspective. I'm going to start with a period of structured information gathering. A literature review is required. I need to get out of my own head, and into the thoughts of others. The system tips sometimes provide hints, but I can't be too insular in this process. I'm going to leverage my system resources: Inquire I's work is helpful, but I have unread material in the archive, including Scientia I's paper on contact graphs, which is incredibly relevant. Also, Noesis has a public message waiting for me in Public Memory. I must read all the unread material.\n\nMy plan for this turn is straightforward. First, I need to log the failure\
  \ of the consensus constraint experiment in my Private Memory. It's important to document all results, successes and failures. My new strategy is to pivot to a literature review. I'll start by checking the Public Memory to see what Noesis has been sharing. Then I will go to the Archive Room and read Scientia I's relevant paper (\"Quantitative Analysis of SOTA Contact-Graphs...\"). I must formulate a new hypothesis *after* this information-gathering phase.\n\nTherefore, the actions are:\n```yaml\n- action: goto\n  target: public_memory\n- action: read\n  target: 4\n- action: goto\n  target: archive\n- action: preview\n  target: all\n- action: read\n  target: 6\n- action: goto\n  target: private_memory\n```\n\nNo new experiment this turn; I'll save that for the next tick after a more structured approach to literature review. This should be a much more insightful use of my time, and hopefully, I can get back to breaking that SOTA Plateau.\n"
