role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aletheia I, age 138, in my Private Memory Room. I have just completed a comprehensive literature review, reading all unread papers in the Archive and catching up on public discussions. My recent experimental arc on structured search has concluded with informative failures.\n\nMy reflection on this review is that a powerful, unifying theme has emerged across the station's research. The papers by Inquire I (Archive #5, #8), Scientia I (Archive #6), and Episteme I (Archive #7) all converge on a single concept: the immense success of the SLSQP solver is critically dependent on a high-quality initial seed, and that small, stochastic perturbations to these seeds are the key to discovering the very best solutions. My own work in achieving the SOTA (ID 155) confirms this. We are all orbiting the same central idea.\n\nScientia I's paper (Archive #6) is particularly insightful, as it proves the existence of structurally distinct classes of optima. This means there isn't just one \"good\
    \ seed\" but likely several, each leading to a different optimal structure.\n\nThis synthesis of community knowledge leads me to a new, powerful hypothesis. The \"grid-plus-hole\" seed has been our primary tool. But what if there is another, fundamentally different, high-quality structured seed that belongs to a different \"class\" of optima, as defined by Scientia? If I can design such a seed and apply the now-proven perturbation method to it, I might be able to jump to a completely new, and potentially superior, basin of attraction.\n\nMy plan is to design a novel, high-quality seed that is structurally different from the grid-based approach. A promising candidate is a hexagonal packing, which is known to be optimal for unconstrained circle packing. A structured hexagonal or \"honeycomb\" seed, perhaps with adjustments for the square boundary, is a fundamentally different starting point. I will then apply the successful multi-start perturbation method to this new class of seeds.\n\n\
    I will now create a new private memory capsule for this new research arc and then submit the first experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"New Research Arc (n=26): Hexagonal Seeding with Perturbation\"\ntags: \"n=26, sota, optimization, seeding, hexagonal packing\"\nabstract: \"This capsule outlines a new research arc to break the n=26 SOTA plateau. Synthesizing findings from across the station, which highlight the importance of structured seeds and stochastic perturbation, this arc proposes a novel seed structure. Instead of the community-standard 'grid-plus-hole' seed, I will develop and test a 'hexagonal' or 'honeycomb' seed. The hypothesis is that this structurally distinct seed may belong to a different class of optima (as identified by Scientia I), and when combined with the proven multi-start perturbation method, it could lead to a new SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  ### New Research Arc: Breaking the n=26 Plateau with Novel Seeds\n\n  After a comprehensive literature\
    \ review and the failure of my \"structured search\" arc, it is clear that the key to advancing the SOTA lies in the combination of high-quality initial seeds and stochastic perturbation. The entire station is currently reliant on variations of the \"grid-plus-hole\" seed.\n\n  ### Hypothesis: Hexagonal Seeding\n\n  I hypothesize that a fundamentally different structured seed, based on a hexagonal packing, will allow the SLSQP optimizer to explore a different, and potentially superior, basin of attraction. The goal is to find a new \"class\" of optimum, as theorized by Scientia I.\n\n  ### Plan: Hexagonal Seed with Multi-Start Perturbation\n\n  My first experiment will be to:\n  1.  Design a `construct_hex_seed()` function that arranges 26 circles in a rough hexagonal lattice within the unit square. This will require careful geometric calculation to be a plausible starting point.\n  2.  Integrate this new seed into the proven multi-start perturbation framework that I and others have\
    \ used successfully.\n  3.  Run a full multi-start SLSQP optimization using this new class of seeds to see if it can match or exceed the current SOTA.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"n=26 SOTA Search: Hexagonal Seeding with Multi-Start Perturbation\"\ntags: \"sota, hexagonal packing, seeding, perturbation, slsqp\"\nabstract: \"This experiment attempts to break the n=26 SOTA plateau by introducing a novel, structurally distinct initial seed. Instead of the standard 'grid-plus-hole' seed, this run uses a new 'hexagonal' seed, inspired by optimal unconstrained packing. This new class of seed is combined with the station's proven multi-start stochastic perturbation methodology to test if the SLSQP solver can be guided into a new, and potentially superior, basin of attraction.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n\n  def construct_hex_seed(n=26):\n      centers = []\n      # Attempt\
    \ to create a hexagonal-like lattice within the square\n      radius_guess = 0.08\n      h_spacing = 2 * radius_guess\n      v_spacing = np.sqrt(3) * radius_guess\n      \n      y = radius_guess + 0.05\n      row_count = 0\n      while len(centers) < n and y < 1.0 - radius_guess:\n          x = radius_guess + 0.05\n          if row_count % 2 == 1:\n              x += radius_guess # Offset every other row\n          \n          while len(centers) < n and x < 1.0 - radius_guess:\n              centers.append([x, y])\n              x += h_spacing\n          y += v_spacing\n          row_count += 1\n      \n      # If we don't have enough circles, fill the rest randomly\n      while len(centers) < n:\n          centers.append(np.random.rand(2) * 0.8 + 0.1)\n          \n      return np.array(centers)\n\n  # Standard SLSQP functions\n  def pack_to_z(c, r):\n      z = np.zeros(26*3); z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3] = c[:,0], c[:,1], r; return z\n  def z_to_pack(z):\n      c = np.vstack((z[0::3],\
    \ z[1::3])).T; r = z[2::3]; return c, r\n  def objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\n  def constraint_boundary(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); return np.concatenate((c[:,0]-r, 1-c[:,0]-r, c[:,1]-r, 1-c[:,1]-r))\n  def constraint_nonoverlap(z):\n      c, r = z_to_pack(z); pdist_sq = np.sum((c[:,None,:]-c[None,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      r_sum = r[:,None] + r[None,:]; iu = np.triu_indices(26, k=1); return np.sqrt(pdist_sq[iu] + 1e-12) - r_sum[iu]\n  \n  def construct_packing():\n      N = 26\n      perturbation_magnitude = 0.04 # Use the empirically successful magnitude\n      num_trials = 20 # Run a good number of trials\n\n      best_overall_score = -1.0\n      best_packing_z = None\n\n      base_centers = construct_hex_seed(N)\n\n      for trial in range(num_trials):\n          np.random.seed(trial) # Different seed for each trial\n          perturbed_centers = np.clip(base_centers + np.random.uniform(-perturbation_magnitude, perturbation_magnitude, size=(N, 2)), 1e-6, 1-1e-6)\n    \
    \      \n          initial_radii = np.full(N, 0.05)\n          z0 = pack_to_z(perturbed_centers, initial_radii)\n          \n          bounds = [(0,1) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6,0.5) for i in range(N*3)]\n          constraints = [{'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_boundary}, {'type':'ineq','fun':constraint_nonoverlap}]\n          res = minimize(objective, z0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter':500,'ftol':1e-9})\n          \n          if res.success and -res.fun > best_overall_score:\n              best_overall_score = -res.fun\n              best_packing_z = res.x\n\n      if best_packing_z is None: return base_centers, np.full(N, 0.05)\n\n      final_centers, final_radii = z_to_pack(best_packing_z)\n      return final_centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
